## Fictionalization and Source Note

This literary paranormal-horror story adapts reported paranormal-investigation and mediumship material into fiction. Names, locations, identities, historical material, and institutional details are fictionalized or invented. The story preserves competing ordinary explanations and unresolved interpretations. Public release excludes raw source materials and restricted participant information.

## Scene 1 — Terms of Return

Diane saw the second tripod and understood that Evan had already scheduled the cleansing.

The basement office door stood open. Black equipment cases lined the narrow strip of floor between the desk and the wall map. Someone had placed them close together, as if they would remain through the evening. A coil of cable sat beneath the coat hooks. Two fresh batteries charged beside the district radio cradle. On Diane’s desk, a printed sheet carried the Bellweather High letterhead above a title Evan had centered in bold type:

FINAL SESSION — BASEMENT CLEARING

The word final had been underlined twice.

Diane stopped in the doorway with her work coat still zipped to her throat. Salt from the entrance mats whitened the edges of her boots. Warm air inside the building began drying the cold damp from her clothes, and the wool at her shoulders gave off a faint smell. Beyond the office, she heard the service corridor’s usual layers: supply air moving through the main duct, water traveling toward the fieldhouse showers, a cart wheel striking the same floor joint every six feet. The next wheel strike arrived where the rhythm placed it. She recognized each sound by sequence. No one had warned her about the equipment in her office.

Evan Kruse knelt beside the larger tripod with a hex key between his fingers. Abby Larson stood at the desk, comparing the clock on her camera with the square facilities clock mounted above the filing cabinet. Neither looked surprised to see her.

Diane took the paper from the desk.

“Who authorized this title?”

Evan tightened the tripod head. “It’s a working title.”

“It describes an outcome.”

“It describes the purpose.”

“The purpose is observation.”

He looked up. Since the first documentary, he had become more deliberate. He spoke more slowly now and used more precise language. “The first project established that something is happening here. This is the follow-up.”

“The first project established disputed recordings, incomplete access, a chair that changed location, and an unidentified key.”

“And a sealed corridor that key opened.”

The fire-inspection addendum had circulated through the activities office after the locksmith’s visit. It recorded that the old key opened the sealed door. It contained no account of the sounds Diane heard beyond the turn.

Diane folded the sheet once through the title. “That remains a facilities matter.”

“It became a facilities matter because you kept the key.”

“I kept custody.”

“That is what I mean.”

Abby turned from the clocks. “The camera is three seconds fast.”

“Correct it,” Diane said.

“I want to record the correction first.”

Diane looked at her.

Abby had brought a spiral notebook, adhesive evidence labels, two empty memory cards in clear sleeves, and a battery log written in the same compact hand she had used during the first investigation. No laptop displayed a music cue. No marker on her page identified a ghost. Diane trusted the preparation, which irritated her because it made permission easier to justify.

“Record both clocks,” Diane said. “State the difference. Then correct the camera and record the new match.”

Abby lifted the camera.

Evan rose. “Miriam will be here at four.”

Diane checked the wall clock.

3:36.

“You scheduled a visitor inside a controlled area before receiving my approval.”

“I sent the request Monday.”

“You sent a message saying you had found someone willing to consult.”

“She had to arrange people.”

“People.”

“One assistant. My mother may come down for the beginning. Miriam doesn’t like walking into a place alone when there are multiple—”

He stopped beneath Diane’s gaze.

“Multiple what?”

“Conditions.”

“That word means nothing until you define it.”

“Presences,” he said. “Possibly.”

A damper changed position, producing a short hollow knock in the supply duct. Evan glanced toward the corridor. Diane marked the glance. He recognized more of the building’s machinery now, yet he still searched each familiar sound for the source of those he could not explain.

Diane set the folded title sheet on the desk. “Who is Miriam Vale?”

“A medium.”

“That is a description of practice. Who is she to you?”

“My mother has known her for years.”

Evan treated the answer as ordinary. Diane heard a possible information route.

“Known her how?”

“Community arts council. Fundraisers. Miriam reads for people too, but that’s separate.”

“Your mother discussed the first investigation with her?”

“I don’t know.”

“You asked?”

“She knows we made a documentary.”

“That was not my question.”

Evan’s mouth tightened. “My mother talks about my work.”

“And about Wayne?”

“No.”

He answered too quickly for Diane to trust it.

Diane crossed to the gray metal cabinet at the end of the workbench beneath the wall map. A combination lock secured the upper door. Only she and Ron held the numbers. She shielded the dial from the camera, entered the combination, and pulled the door wide enough for Abby’s camera to see the interior without recording the lock.

Three evidence envelopes rested on the middle shelf. The largest held the unidentified brass key. Its surface had stayed dry since the sealed corridor opened, though the mineral line around the bow remained. Beside it lay the corridor photographs and a copy of the locksmith’s report. The third envelope contained a sample of paint and corrosion from the old lock.

Diane touched the key envelope with two fingers.

“Record the seal number,” she told Abby.

Abby read it aloud. The number matched Diane’s log.

Evan watched from behind her shoulder. “You think tonight connects to that.”

“I think you think it connects.”

“Something followed Wayne’s route to a door nobody had opened in decades.”

“A series of events resembled a route after we arranged them in that order.”

“The key opened the door.”

“That event stands on its own.”

“And what happened after you opened it?”

Diane closed the upper door.

She spun the dial. The lock clicked once.

“That account remains restricted.”

“You told Ron.”

“Ron was part of the work order.”

“You recorded it.”

“For the work order.”

“So it exists.”

Diane turned. “Existence grants you no automatic right of use.”

Both remembered the first review: labels replaced, names removed, raw files restored beside edited versions. Evan had accepted every release condition and then incorporated those conditions into the documentary. Viewers read the disclosures as honesty, and many treated the preserved uncertainty as evidence that the strongest material had survived the review.

Abby lowered the camera. “We need a new agreement before anyone enters.”

“We need no session,” Diane said.

Evan gestured toward the cases. “Then Miriam leaves, my mother tells the principal that facilities blocked an approved consultant, and this happens later without a clean record. The first film left them with conflicting accounts of what had happened. I want one record of what we tried after it.”

“You are using an unsupervised return as leverage.”

“I am telling you the practical outcome.”

The equipment reduced the remaining floor space. Diane considered the routes Evan could use without her. He could enter the auditorium during rehearsal. Staff, coaches, contractors, and student crews shared access to several basement doors. A phone in a pocket could produce footage without clocks, positions, or custody. She could stop this afternoon and lose control of every later return.

Footsteps approached from the service corridor.

Ron Meier appeared at the doorway carrying a box of air filters against his chest. He took in the tripods, the cases, Evan’s expression, and the folded title sheet on Diane’s desk. His face settled before he spoke.

“No.”

Evan spread his hands. “You don’t know the procedure.”

“I know the basement.”

“Miriam helps people leave places.”

Ron shifted the filter box against one hip. “Then she can help you leave this one.”

Abby looked down at her notebook.

Diane said, “Nothing has been approved.”

Ron’s attention moved to her. “The equipment is inside your office.”

“I found it here.”

“And they are still here.”

Evan stepped toward him. “You won’t be on camera unless you agree.”

“I won’t be in the room.”

“We need the person with the breathing account.”

“You have my written statement from the first case. It ends where it ends.”

“Miriam might explain what happened to you.”

Ron’s grip tightened against the cardboard. “My account is not a symptom for somebody else to diagnose.”

His wording repeated his earlier refusal almost exactly. Diane remembered the stopped basement test, the failed camera battery, and how the group had replayed the recording and heard different words. She had apologized afterward for treating his fear as another condition to record. He had accepted the apology while preserving the boundary.

You set the end point. Then you honor it.

Evan turned to Diane. “He can leave. That doesn’t stop an investigation.”

Ron answered before she could. “A cleansing is not an investigation.”

“That’s why we’re recording it.”

“Recording expands the audience. It adds no proof.”

Evan gave a short exhale. “You both act like documentation causes events.”

“No,” Ron said. “Documentation causes ownership.”

The office went quiet except for the camera fan and the charger’s faint electrical hum.

Ron set the filter box on the floor. He looked at Diane rather than Evan. “You supervise this, you choose it.”

“I can limit it.”

“You limited the booth test.”

Ron stated the fact without accusation.

Diane held his gaze.

He continued. “You put your name on the door, the visitor passes, the keys, the cameras, the end time. After that, supervision is not neutrality. When somebody crosses a line, it is your line they crossed because you opened the room.”

